fix(memory): keep every record that can still find a file, and refuse a path Compose would rewrite

Round 20 review, GPT-5.6 Terra. Six findings, all on paths this round
touched.

A decision retires the manifest row for the file it removed, and the same
bytes can be under a reserved name an earlier attempt could not drop.
Rejection, promotion and both profile-apply paths now take those copies
with them, and report the file as not gone when they cannot -- so the row
is kept and marked instead of retired over a copy nothing can name.

The bytes a decision acts on are not always the bytes the row was created
with: a user may edit a proposal in their vault before accepting it. A
tidying that fails now records what it actually acted on, so the row can
still prove ownership; bound to the words Turing first proposed it would
refuse forever and the session would never finish deleting.

An entry the residue sweep cannot read is not evidence that a file is
gone, so it refuses instead of reporting a clear vault, and the bound is
on what it reads rather than on what it happens to match.

A vault whose root cannot be opened is not an absence. Reporting one as
"the file is gone" retires every record naming every note on a disk
nobody has attached. Every absence that retires a record now goes through
the primitive that proves it, including the crash-recovery path for an
applied profile edit.

And a display root that is not single-quoted is refused before launch:
Compose interpolates bare and double-quoted values, so a line left by an
older install can name TURING_INTEGRATION_KEY and reach the Memory page
as that key.

Two findings are answered rather than changed, and are written down in
the governance document: a promotion whose original cannot be put back
keeps the belief it wrote, and the sweep can only be raced by a writer
staging byte-identical content, which for this package means the same
note identity and so the same session.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
